From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 21 May 94 20:30:39 GMT
Subject: [#94-5-257] insurance, risk-pools, freedom

R. David Murray replies:
> I see no reason to use retalitory force.  In a fully free society the 
> person with no insurance and no money to pay off the debt adjudicated by 
> a mutually agreed upon arbitration board has two choices: work to pay off 
> that debt, or abrogate that contract.  A person who abrogates such a 
> contract (the contract here is between them, the arbitration board, and 
> the other party to the dispute) is going to be in a very poor position in 
> a free society, a society that of necessity depends heavily on reputation.

Your model of low abrogation rates, with "uninsured insurance" costs being
less than enforcement costs, would be a better goal than my "retaliatory
force" society.  To make the fully free society work, we'd have to ensure
that the cost of dropping out of normal society (considering all the pros
and cons of joining an outlaw community) is greater than the cost to the
offender of working to repay the debt.  Outlaws in pre-nanotech society
could achieve a reasonable standard of living given sufficient population
size and diversity of skills, so the deterrent of ostracism would start at
a maximum and then fall.  We could try ostracism first, and if the outlaw
community grows too large, then revert to retaliatory force (and possible
warfare!).  The retaliatory force society could always be part of a
transitional strategy if it proves more popular.

During the period when the outlaw community is still quite small, smugglers
who export goods to the abrogators could prove very damaging to their own
PPL.  And if someone supplies a Net connection, large amounts of digital
cash could be amassed from BlackNet-style contract killings, blackmail,
etc.  But the 'gators would be cheating excessively amongst themselves,
would be greatly outnumbered, and likely to be lagging in terms of
technological developments and innovations.

There was a good post on Libernet recently by Carol Moore, on secessionist
strategies, and it described the principle of community autonomy.  Examples
were given of alternative communities' interests, and the laws that would
be associated with each.  I predict that after the collapse of statism, a
large number of PPLs will exist.  As folk converge on the type of community
they seek, a diversity of communities based on various ideals will "spring
up" all over the place.  Let's say one community believes in laws
prohibiting certain victimless crimes, e.g. speeding, carrying guns, drugs,
and pornography.  If someone from a "no-victimless crimes" PPL / community
passes through and commits such a "crime", is she liable?  I suppose it'd
depend on which community owns the land.

Relations between these communities would be poor if the "victimless  
crimes" PPL's jurisdiction extended over a very long, narrow strip of land,
over a considerable width of a continent or island, for instance, and it
was difficult to avoid passing through.  I'd suggest that any "victimless
crimes" community confined its jurisdiction to residential areas and nature
reserves, with the freeways and main routes "victimless crime law free"
zones.  On the approaches to a "victimless crime" community, large signs,
of a standard nature and easily recognisable, would detail lists of crimes
that outsiders could be charged with, together with the penalties for
contravention.  There would be lay-bys for travellers to pull in and read
each list.  Local people, if they elected, could be governed by so many
laws that it would take thousands of pages to write them all down, but
members of alternative PPLs would get immunity from all laws apart from
those listed on the roadsigns.

(In the case of "victim crimes" such as murder, assault, theft and
vandalism, the default would be that these crimes were universally
recognised unless otherwise specified, e.g. in the case of a hedonistic
society.  The approach roads to these communities would need to have a
minimum of, say, three large roadsigns depicting a skull and crossbones,
and there would be plenty of publicity so that everyone knew about these
zones.  TV reporters could be guaranteed safe passage to film killings.
More mildly hedonistic PPLs would revert to the default rule, that people
could only be killed after having given permission -- e.g. on video, with
three witnesses.)

An interesting case would be that of a community where property crime had
become such a problem that drastic measures were being taken to stamp it
out, such as torture and corporal punishment.  Or maybe some victimless
crime became perceived as a terrible problem that must be fought against
with Draconian penalties.  If the penalties also applied to members of
other PPLs and the risk of wrongful conviction appeared significant, such
a community could be viewed as a no-go zone by freedom dwellers!

