Subject: [#94-5-259] Visit to England

Elizabeth Schwartz, replying to R. David Murray:
>Doesn't England have 220Volt, 50 cycle current? It's not enough to 
>convert the voltage; electronic equipment also wants 60Hz power. 
>(some high-end laptops are designed to operate overseas but most are not.)

It's 240V 50Hz.  Some equipment might have a recessed slide switch or
jumpers which are screwed in place, or there could be jumpers inside that
select between 240V 50Hz and 110/120V 60Hz.

>There are also different standards for modems, but I don't know about
>that. 1200 or 2400 requires changing a carrier tone , and I've seen some
>low-end modems do this (I've used a US modem to *phone* England, but not
>recently.) I'd hope that higher speeds are more compatible, does anyone
>know? 

At 2400 bps and faster, the CCITT protocols are used everywhere; at 300
and 1200 bps the U.S. and Canada use the Bell protocols, and Europe and
nearly everywhere else use CCITT.

>[Also in more socialist countries there are laws about plugging stuff into
>the phone system without paying $$$ to the governments...this is true in at
>least Germany and the Netherlands]

In the U.K. some modems are approved for connection, and have green
stickers to prove it; others are non-approved and connection is illegal
(but generally works fine, I hear).

