From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Sat, 21 May 1994 22:29:11 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-260] Rights of Orangutangs

 freeman@netcom.com  (Jay Reynolds Freeman) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-229:
>  A most intersting approach to the subject, of particular appeal to the First
>Extropian Squirrel, since he is very good at loud complaints.  He notes that
>with this line of reasoning, "animal rights" folk have hope of establishing
>that non-human-animal rights are legitimate, not by persuading human animals
>tht other animals "ought" to have rights, but rather by learning about the
>behavior and communicative skills of non-human animals, and using that
>knowledge to convince humans that complaints are being made.

 There is a potentially serious drawback to this which seems to weaken my
argument significantly. Suppose that a group of animal rights activists
spends a lot of time and money, and then announces that some orangutangs
(or squirrels or earthworms) have communicated breach-of-contract
complaints to them, and if you just read this 1-gigabyte research report,
you will be able to understand how we achieved this communication.
Suddenly we are back to the problem that there is no easy way to decide
whether an entity has standing in our judicial systems.

 On the internet, nobody can tell whether you are an orangutang or an
animal rights activist pretending to be an orangutang.
