From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Sun, 22 May 1994 11:20:07 -0700
Subject: [#94-5-263] Rights of Orangutangs

Peter McCluskey opines:
 
> ... potentially serious drawback ... if you just read this 1-gigabyte
> research report ...
 
  Alas, no one said that questions relating to the perceptions and
communications of non-human animals were going to be easy to resolve; thus
far in the history of ethology, they certainly haven't been.  Several
approaches to circumventing the drawback proposed by McCluskey are
in principle possible, though:
 
  (1) Consideration of the reputation (or lack of it) of the researchers.
  (2) Application of the scientific method in general.  There will certainly
      be plenty of people who will be willing, able and eager to read that
      report, and to see if its findings can be duplicated independently.
  (3) Use of modern techical means to simplify and understand communication;
      examples of such means that have already been used include:
 
      (a) Audio frequency multipliers that "transpose" (not quite, but
          close enough for brief description) between the audio ranges
          of human voices and those of porpoise utterances,
      (b) Use of computers to provide specialized, well-monitored
          communication interfaces (like Koko's Mac II, which I mentioned
          in a previous post), and
      (c) Correct use of modern statistical techniques to investigate
          correlations between behaviors and utterances.
 
                                          --  Jay Freeman,
                                              First Extropian Squirrel

